    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201961707923_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Adult Education Instructor/English Language Learners (ELL)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary The AEL/ESL Teacher is responsible for providing high-quality instruction to adult learners seeking to improve their academic skills, earn a High School Equivalency (HiSET) credential, and develop English language proficiency.     This position supports student achievement by creating an inclusive and learner-centered classroom environment, delivering effective instruction in reading, mathematics, language arts, and English as a Second Language (ESL), and using assessment data to guide instruction and monitor student progress.   The AEL/ESL Teacher helps adult learners overcome educational barriers, build confidence, develop workforce and life skills, and achieve their academic, career, and personal goals while maintaining compliance with Missouri Adult Education and Literacy (AEL) program. Essential Job Functions, Duties, & Responsibilities: The AEL/ESL Teacher is responsible for providing instructional services to adult learners seeking to improve academic skills, achieve a High School Equivalency (HiSET) credential, and develop English language proficiency.  The position plans, prepares, and delivers effective instruction in reading, mathematics, language arts, and English as a Second Language (ESL) using evidence-based teaching strategies that accommodate diverse learning styles and educational backgrounds.  The instructor creates and maintains a positive, inclusive, and learner-centered classroom environment that promotes student engagement, persistence, and success. Responsibilities include assessing student skill levels, monitoring academic progress, maintaining accurate attendance and performance records, and adapting instructional method. Additional Duties and Responsibilities In addition to instructional responsibilities, the AEL/ESL Teacher may assist with student recruitment, orientation, intake, and goal-setting activities to support enrollment, retention, and student success.  The instructor may participate in outreach efforts, special events, and community partnerships that promote Adult Education and Literacy (AEL) services and English language learning opportunities.  Responsibilities may also include supporting distance education and hybrid learning initiatives, integrating educational technology into instruction, assisting students with career exploration and workforce readiness activities, and making referrals to appropriate support services when needed.  The AEL/ESL Teacher may collaborate with colleagues on curriculum development, instructional material selection, data collection, grant-related activities. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: The successful candidate must possess knowledge of adult learning principles, instructional strategies, classroom management techniques, and educational assessment practices.  The candidate should demonstrate strong communication, organizational, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills, with the ability to effectively teach and motivate adult learners from diverse educational, cultural, and language backgrounds. The position requires the ability to adapt instruction to meet individual student needs, utilize educational technology and digital learning tools, maintain accurate records, and analyze student performance data to support academic achievement.  The ideal candidate will exhibit professionalism, integrity, patience, empathy, cultural sensitivity, dependability, and a strong commitment to student success.  Additionally, the candidate must be able to work collaboratively with colleagues and community partners while fostering a positive, inclusive, and supportive learning environment that encourages lifelong learning and personal growth. Minimum Qualifications: High School Diploma, GED, HiSET, or equivalent required. Certifications, Licenses, Registrations: The successful candidate must possess a valid bachelor's degree from an accredited institution and be eligible to obtain and maintain certification as an Adult Education and Literacy (AEL) Instructor in accordance with Missouri Department of Elementary and Secondary Education (DESE) requirements.  Candidates must complete all required Missouri AEL professional development and certification requirements within the timeframe established by DESE and the university, typically within four months of hire.  Additional certifications related to Adult Education, English as a Second Language (ESL), workforce readiness, instructional technology, or teaching credentials are preferred and may be required as program needs evolve. Physical Demands: This position requires the ability to sit, stand, walk, bend, reach, communicate effectively, and operate standard office and instructional technology.  The employee may occasionally lift and carry materials weighing up to 25 pounds and must be able to work at a computer for extended periods.  Reasonable accommodations may be made for qualified individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions of the job. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201961589925_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Grounds Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-03\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary The Grounds Manager is responsible for the leadership, planning, coordination, and daily operation of all campus grounds and landscape maintenance activities. This position oversees the appearance, safety, and functionality of the university's outdoor environments, including lawns, athletic fields, landscaped areas, irrigation systems, parking lots, sidewalks, exterior waste management, and pest control programs. The Grounds Manager serves as a working supervisor who actively participates in field operations while directing staff, contractors, and seasonal personnel.  Essential Job Function, Duties and Responsibilities: Plan, supervise, and participate in the maintenance of campus grounds, landscaping, turf, trees, shrubs, and flower beds Develop and implement annual grounds maintenance, mowing, irrigation, fertilization, and landscape improvement plans.  Create and manage mowing schedules and seasonal grounds maintenance programs.  Supervise grounds personnel, assign work orders, conduct training, and evaluate employee performance.  Manage and coordinate contracted grounds, landscaping, snow removal, tree service, and pest control vendors.  Develop and administer turf management programs, including fertilization, aeration, overseeding, weed control, and disease prevention.  Maintain required pesticide and herbicide applicator licenses and ensure all applications comply with applicable regulations.  Perform and oversee pesticide, herbicide, and integrated pest management activities. Manage exterior trash collection and campus cleanliness programs.  Inspect campus grounds and identify maintenance needs, safety hazards, and opportunities for improvement.  Assist with budgeting, equipment replacement planning, and procurement of grounds-related supplies and services.  Maintain irrigation systems and coordinate repairs as needed.  Respond to weather-related emergencies and assist with snow and ice removal operations.  Operate grounds maintenance equipment including mowers, tractors, utility vehicles, sprayers, skid steers, and related equipment.  Maintain records related to pesticide applications, equipment maintenance, inspections, and regulatory compliance.  Support campus events through grounds preparation, setup, and cleanup activities. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Thorough knowledge of turfgrass management, horticulture, landscaping practices, and grounds maintenance techniques.  Knowledge of pesticide, herbicide, fertilizer, and integrated pest management programs.  Knowledge of irrigation systems, drainage systems, and landscape construction.  Ability to supervise, train, motivate, and evaluate employees.  Ability to develop maintenance schedules and prioritize work activities.  Ability to read and interpret landscape plans, specifications, and contracts.  Knowledge of OSHA safety requirements and safe equipment operation.  Strong communication and customer service skills.  Ability to manage multiple projects and contractors simultaneously.  Proficiency with computers, work order systems, and Microsoft Office applications.  Ability to prepare reports, budgets, and maintenance plans. Minimum Qualifications: Associate's degree in Turf Management, Horticulture, Landscape Management, Agriculture, or related field. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Turf Management, Horticulture, Landscape Management, Agriculture, or related field.  Certified Grounds Manager, Certified Sports Field Manager, or similar professional certification.  Experience managing grounds operations for a university, municipality, school district, park system, golf course, or large commercial campus.  Experience with irrigation system design and maintenance.  Commercial Driver's License (CDL).  Experience preparing and managing departmental budgets.  Physical Demands: Frequently required to stand, walk, bend, kneel, climb, lift, carry, push, and pull materials and equipment.    Ability to lift up to 75 pounds regularly and greater weights with assistance.  Ability to work outdoors in varying weather conditions including heat, cold, rain, wind, and snow.    Ability to safely operate grounds maintenance equipment and vehicles.    Ability to work evenings, weekends, and emergency response situations as required.    Ability to traverse uneven terrain and perform inspections throughout campus facilities and grounds.  Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201943422567_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Records Support Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-30\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: The Records Support Specialist will handle public inquiries, review, and route incoming correspondence, manage other front office duties, assist the faculty with the curriculum change process, and work with the Athletics division on NCAA eligibility requirements. Essential Job Functions: Duties as office coordinator, ordering supplies, typing and filing correspondence, scheduling meetings and appointments, preparing and maintaining office records, reports, and correspondence.  Assist the public with inquiries. Answer incoming calls Maintain and update student files.  Data entry  Assist LU faculty with navigating the curriculum update process.  Complete enrollment verifications.  Verify student athlete eligibility.  Helping with student requests (major changes, schedule changes, address updates, etc.)  Sending student correspondence (letters and emails)  Operate computer and/or processors in preparing restriction letters, correspondence, prospective, graduation lists, weekly reports, and other reports as needed.  Operate a variety of modern office equipment in performing routine tasks such as facsimile machines, copy machines, calculators, computer terminals, and phone systems.  Deliver and pick up mail.  Submit and track requisitions and purchases for Registrar’s office.  Performs special projects as needed or requested.    Exhibits student-centeredness in the performance of all job duties. Anticipates, listens to, understands, and responds to customer needs. Delivers work products and services to customers in a way that reflects positively upon the department and the University. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Must be able to prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment.  Excellent interpersonal and verbal communication skills.    Excellent organizational and time-management skills.   Strong analytical and problem-solving skills.    Ability to work within a culturally diverse environment.  Work performed in a general office environment Minimum Qualifications: High School Diploma or equivalent.   Preferred Qualification: Associate degree from an accredited college or university and experience in higher education desired.   Physical Demands: Nature of work requires an ability to operate standard business office equipment.  Light sedentary office work    Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer   Must be able to lift up to 25 lbs.   May require overtime hours depending on workload on occasion during peak periods     Travel on occasion may be required as it relates to position’s responsibilities. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201924438998_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Access and Abilities Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary  The Coordinator for Access and Abilities Services plays a pivotal role in supporting student retention and academic success by ensuring equitable access to the learning environment in alignment with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) and related regulations. Reporting to the Senior Director of Student Health & Well-being, this position is responsible for the administration of accommodation services, promoting awareness and inclusion, and fostering collaborative partnerships across campus to support students with disabilities. The coordinator oversees intake, documentation review, accommodation planning, and case management while educating students, faculty, and staff on best practices for accessibility and inclusive learning.  Essential Job Functions, Duties and Responsibilities  Serve as the primary point of contact for students seeking academic and housing accommodations under ADA and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act.  Oversee the intake process, including reviewing documentation, assessing eligibility, and developing individualized accommodation plans.  Draft and disseminate Letters of Accommodation to students while ensuring timely communication and support throughout the semester.  Provide direct support and mentoring to students, focusing on areas such as study skills, time management, writing, and class attendance.  Facilitate the Emotional Support Animal (ESA) intake and approval process in collaboration with Residence Life and other campus partners.  Act as a liaison between students and faculty to mediate concerns and ensure appropriate implementation of approved accommodations.  Design and deliver presentations and trainings to students, parents, faculty, and staff that promote the mission of the AAS office and raise awareness about accessibility and inclusion.  Participate in new student orientations, campus events, and outreach efforts to increase visibility of services and encourage early engagement.  Coordinate and facilitate workshops and study sessions on academic success strategies, note-taking, test preparation, and other topics tailored to student needs.    Attend departmental and faculty meetings to advocate for inclusive teaching practices and enhance campus-wide understanding of disability services.  Supervise and maintain the AAS testing center, ensuring secure and appropriate testing accommodations.  Support front desk operations and general office functions within the Office of Student Health & Well-being as needed.      Collect and analyze data to assess program effectiveness, student engagement, and accommodation trends to inform strategic planning.  Assist in developing and revising departmental policies and procedures to remain compliant with federal and state regulations.  Enter and maintain accurate and confidential student accommodation records in the Medicat Database, ensuring compliance with FERPA and ADA documentation standards.    Perform other duties as assigned. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Working knowledge of ADA laws/regulations as pertaining to higher education.    Working knowledge of Microsoft Office products (Word, PowerPoint, Excel).    Ability to mediate between students and faculty.    Willingness to stay current on new laws and/or changes to ADA laws, court cases, etc.    Ability to maintain/organize student records.   Strong interpersonal and communication skills with the ability to build trust and maintain confidentiality.  Demonstrated cultural competency and a commitment to supporting diverse student populations.  Strong organizational, analytical, and time-management skills; ability to manage multiple priorities effectively.  Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite; experience with database and case management systems is a plus.  Ability to collaborate across departments and serve as an advocate for inclusive practices.  High attention to detail with a student-centered approach to service delivery.  Ability to work independently and as part of a team in a fast-paced, dynamic environment.  Flexibility to work occasional evenings and weekends to support programming and outreach. Minimum Qualifications · High School Diploma or GED required.  · Three years of administrative, clerical, office support, or related experience. Preferred Qualifications · Experience in higher education or academic office environment.  · Experience using student information systems, enterprise systems, or similar administrative software. ·Physical Demands Light office work with prolonged periods of sitting and computer use.  Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. as needed for event or presentation materials. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201924450188_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student Support Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary  The Student Support Coordinator for graduate and extended studies/non-traditional programs provides student centered support for recruitment, inquiry response, application assistance, admissions coordination, onboarding, and student persistence.  This position serves as a primary point of contact for prospective, applicant, admitted, and enrolled students; supports students as they move through the inquiry, application, admission, enrollment, and onboarding process; maintains communication with academic departments and campus partners; and provides data related to inquiries, applications, admissions, enrollment, retention, and persistence to support enrollment growth and planning.  The position supports programs within graduate studies and extended/non-traditional academic programs as assigned, including, but not limited to, certificate programs, dual credit, Adult Education & Literacy, Learning in Retirement, and other related academic initiatives. Essential Job Functions, Duties and Responsibilities  Serve as a primary student support contact for prospective, applicant, admitted, and enrolled students in graduate, extended studies, and related non-traditional academic programs.   Respond to questions from prospective and current applicants regarding program options, admissions requirements, application steps, deadlines, missing materials, and next steps.    Track student application progress from inquiry through admission, maintain accurate records of contacts and application status, and communicate concerns, delays, or barriers to appropriate offices and program leaders.    Coordinate onboarding for admitted and enrolled students, including communication, orientation support, next-step guidance, and connection to appropriate university resources.    Provide application follow-up and proactive outreach to students with incomplete applications, missing admissions materials, or unresolved enrollment steps.    Monitor student engagement, enrollment status, and retention concerns; communicate issues to appropriate departments, program leaders, and campus offices.    Serve as a liaison between students, Admissions, Student Financial Services, Registrar, academic departments, and Graduate and Extended Studies.    Support recruitment, marketing, outreach, information sessions, events, and table presence for graduate, extended studies, non-traditional, and certificate programs in collaboration with academic departments, Admissions, and Marketing.    Provide application, inquiry, admissions, recruitment, retention, and persistence data to support enrollment planning and decision-making.    Identify barriers in the student intake, admissions, onboarding, enrollment, and retention process and recommend improvements to strengthen student service and enrollment outcomes.    Maintain confidentiality and comply with university policies related to student records, admissions information, and student support documentation.    Perform other duties as assigned.  Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of higher education admissions, recruitment, student services, and enrollment processes.    Ability to communicate clearly and professionally with prospective students, applicants, departments, and university offices.    Strong attention to detail, follow-through, organization, problem-solving, and commitment to student centered service. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree preferred. One to three years of experience in higher education, admissions, recruitment, student services, academic advising, enrollment management, or related administrative support. Experience communicating with students, applicants, faculty, staff, and campus partners.   Experience using student information systems, CRM systems, application systems, or similar technology platforms preferred.    Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, organizational, and customer service skills.    Ability to track multiple students, deadlines, application steps, and communication needs with accuracy.    Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a fast-paced environment.   Physical Demands Prolonged periods of sitting and computer use. Light sedentary office work. Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201924478383_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Director Student Conduct & Community Standards",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-19\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nPosition Assistant Director Student Conduct & Community Standards  Job Summary  The Assistant Director for Student Conduct and Community Standards serves as a key administrative leader within the Division of Student Affairs and plays a vital role in fostering a safe, respectful, and educational campus environment. Reporting to the Assistant Dean, Student Conduct & Community Standards the Assistant Director provides direct support in the administration, coordination, and enhancement of the University's student conduct system. This position works collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and administrators to address behavioral concerns, promote accountability, and support student development both on and off campus.  The Assistant Director is responsible for developing and delivering educational outreach, training programs, and conduct procedures that promote student understanding of their rights and responsibilities as outlined in the Code of Student Conduct. This position also plays an essential role in supporting equity, inclusion, and access for a diverse student population, utilizing trauma-informed and developmentally appropriate approaches in all student interactions.  Through data-informed decision-making, strategic partnerships, and a strong commitment to restorative practices, the Assistant Director supports the university’s mission to foster a community grounded in integrity, accountability, and student success.  Essential Job Functions, Duties & Responsibilities  Receives, investigates, and monitors bias incident reports involving students; engages in developmentally focused conversations to resolve issues.  Provides strategic direction and oversight for student conduct and student code of conduct policies, ensuring alignment with institutional goals and compliance requirements.  Trains and monitors campus judicial officers using a distributive conduct model; recruits, trains, and advises hearing boards.  Oversees Project Success  Collaborates with campus partners to assess and implement educational programming addressing alcohol and drug use, sexual misconduct, interpersonal conflict, and safety education.  Educates students on rights and responsibilities through active and passive educational programming.  Serve as a point of contact for current and potential students, parents, and other university administrators regarding the Code of Conduct, the SCCS office, and related services.  Assist the Assistant Dean in developing and facilitating training for the Disciplinary Hearing Board each semester.  Assist with administrative support of the office such as coordinating and preparing for disciplinary hearings, sending decision letters, and scheduling hearings.   Work collaboratively with the Communications team to create and enhance publications, brochures, manuals, and other resource materials regarding the office.  With guidance from the Assistant Dean, lead assessment efforts for the office as well as maintain detailed statistical data for monthly and yearly reports and Clery data.  Oversee programming and prevention initiatives surrounding community standards, the Code of Conduct, and other student behavioral trends. Lead the development and implementation of SCCS programs and workshops in collaboration with Housing and Residential Education.  Participate in regular training for Student Conduct, Title IX, and other professional development opportunities.  Serves as a resource to campus partners (e.g., residence life, wellness, academic programs) in planning and implementing student-focused initiatives.  Provides frequent, data-driven reports on departmental progress to campus and community stakeholders.  Aligns departmental strategy with university-wide initiatives and fosters collaboration with academic and administrative partners.  Adapts leadership strategies based on emerging data and facilitates unified movement toward new directions. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Thorough knowledge of Maxient, StarRez software, Adobe Suite and proficient in using Microsoft Office suite.  Knowledge of Title IX, Title VII, Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), Clery Act, Drug-Free Schools and Community Act (DFSCA), and Office of Civil Rights compliance  Excellent organizational and time-management skills.  Strong analytical and problem-solving skills.  Must prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment, develop communication strategies and work with internal and external stakeholders.  Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills.  Ability to work within a culturally diverse environment.  Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with high accuracy.  A high degree of organization, ability to engage in various tasks simultaneously, and consistently meet deadlines.  Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information.  Ability to adapt to a flexible schedule that includes evening and weekend activities.  Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's Degree from an accredited institution.  Experience and ability to manage budgets.  Experience in student conduct and an awareness of emerging issues in higher education, residence life and housing, community building, and student learning.  Proven competence in leadership and supervision of students and staff.  Physical Demands Light sedentary office work.  Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer.  Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201872552816_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director of Sponsored Programs and Title III",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-31\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: The Director of Sponsored Programs and Title III assists administrators, faculty, and staff with identifying funding opportunities, planning, writing, submitting, managing, coordinating, and overseeing the pre-award life cycle through grant awards. Ensure deliverables, in conjunction with the assigned staff per grant awards and terms and conditions. Position will supervise the Grant Coordinator and Title III Coordinator.  Coordination with financial staff will be required both in the pre- and post-award life cycle of grants. Additionally, the position will support Title III programs in pre-award efforts and with post-award program management, working in conjunction with the Comptroller to ensure both pre- and post-award requirements are met, and projects are implemented on time and within their approved budgets.  Essential Job Functions, Duties & Responsibilities: Assist faculty and staff in the different departments within the University in successfully navigating both formula and competitive grant processes including identifying sources of external funding from federal, state and private agencies, manage grant development and submission, manage pre-award process, and disseminate grant documents to Comptroller’s office for post-award financial processes (including Title III and USDA capacity funds).  Develop and maintain relationships with external grant agencies, as well as other administrative offices and departments within the University.  Conduct grant workshops and training sessions for administrators, faculty and staff on grant development and administration.  Develop the Office of Research Support and Sponsored Programs annual report.   Develop, monitor, and maintain a grant tracking system to ensure timely preparation of grant applications and implementation of grant requirements.    Maintain an application tracking and award spreadsheet that allows all funding outcomes to be tracked by academic/program area and by individual faculty along with the overall success rate and total dollars awarded to LU annually.  For projects that are not funded, whenever possible, request copies of the grant reviewer’s comments and share with the application team at LU. This information should be used to determine if a viable grant can and should be submitted next year.  Funding opportunity reports should be created for priority areas with the greatest funding needs. Grant forecasts should also be created and shared with deans, provost, and program administrators at least annually. This should include a summary of the opportunity and general due dates and average award size and match requirements.   Will supervise Grant Coordinator and Title III Coordinator.  Coordinate Title III program(s) as well as USDA capacity funds and ensure grant program reporting is completed timely by grantor agency deadlines.  Serve as the authorized representative for the University in signing documents related to grant application proposal processes and for program reporting and collaborating with fiscal staff on fiscal reporting as needed.  Support project directors to ensure program goals, guidelines, policies, and procedures are met. Serve as Title III point of contact for university for communications with U.S. Department of Education.    Coordinate, monitor and report on all Title III funded activities as required by university administrators and by the US Department of Education. Identify and report problems to administration as soon as possible to mitigate risk and ensure projects stay on track.  Support the University Land Grant programs to ensure timely submission of grants and help ensure timely reporting to avoid program or audit findings.  Maintain knowledge, skills, and abilities regarding both competitive and formula grants and ensure the university is kept informed of opportunities or administrative changes that affect external funding programs.    Other duties as assigned.  Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Excellent written and oral communication skills. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Must be deadline orientated, flexible, and extremely organized.  Excellent people skills to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and administrators.  One year of experience with Microsoft Office.  Familiarity with Grants.gov and other widely used grant systems.  Qualifications:  Bachelor’s degree required or five plus years’ experience in higher education or nonprofit organization. Bachelor’s degree requirement will be waived with five plus years’ experience.  Five or more years of grant related experience required.  Demonstrated ability to write successful grant proposals and managing complex processes.    Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree or seven plus years of experience in higher education or nonprofit organization. One-year supervisory experience preferred. Significant grant training experience with a professional grant certification.  One year of experience with Microsoft Office.    Experience in Colleague system.  Physical Demands: Light sedentary office work.  Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer.  Ability to lift to 25 lbs.  Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201871852154_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Residence Hall Director & Residential Education Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-29\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary:   The Residence Hall Director & Residential Education Coordinator is a live-in, full-time professional staff member within the Division of Student Affairs, serving as a dynamic leader in the residential community. This dual role position focuses on managing the overall operations of a residence hall while coordinating residential education initiatives. Responsibilities include supervising student staff, developing community-building programs, crisis management, policy enforcement, and fostering an inclusive environment that supports academic success and personal development. This position combines responsibilities for hall management and staff supervision with leadership in student learning and development through a residential curriculum framework.  Reporting to the Assistant Dean for Residence Life and Student Development, the position manages one to two residential buildings, supervises 5–12 Resident Assistants, implements residential programming model and designs intentional learning experiences that foster student success, retention, and personal growth. The role also supports the university’s strategic priorities through innovative programming, academic interventions, crisis response, and inclusive practices that promote a vibrant living-learning environment.  Essential Job Functions: Residential Community Leadership and Management Provide strategic leadership and oversight for residential communities aligned with university goals. Ensure residential facilities meet health, safety, and security standards.  Promote an inclusive community that supports the needs of underrepresented student populations.  Oversee the daily operations of one to two residence halls.  Assist in hall security and facility management efforts, including key control and health/safety inspections. Student Staff Supervision and Development Supervise, mentor, and evaluate a staff of 5 – 12 Resident Assistants: lead staff training, development, and team-building activities.  Conduct weekly staff meetings and one-on-one check-ins; ensure alignment with departmental goals and student needs.  Support staff in programming efforts, conflict resolution, academic support, and crisis management. Residential Curriculum and Educational Initiatives     Lead department and campus-wide residential education initiatives, including large-scale events, targeted programming, and pop-up engagement.   Implement and assess residential curriculum goals and co-curricular strategies, ensuring alignment with the university’s mission and student affairs learning outcomes.  Train housing staff on educational outcomes, programming assessment, academic interventions, and inclusive excellence.  Meet regularly with Resident Assistants and learning community partners to support the execution of community development and learning plans. Academic Engagement and Support Coordinate academic intervention strategies in conjunction with University College for residents and staff, including outreach to students in academic distress and connection to campus resources  Develop academic recognition initiatives for high-performing residents each semester.  Develop and structure Living Learning Communities within the department. Student Conduct and Community Standards Serve as an administrative hearing officer for conduct violations related to residence life and housing policies.  Educate residents about community standards, policies, and their rights and responsibilities.  Maintain accurate conduct records and submit timely reports using the Maxient conduct management system, including instances such as students of concern, student behavioral issues, sexual misconduct allegations, and other relevant student conduct matters.  Advise and support residents in their academic, social, and personal development. Crisis Intervention and Student Support Provide crisis intervention and support to residents, making referrals to the CARE team or other support offices as appropriate.  Participate in a rotating on-call duty schedule that includes after-hours, weekend, and holiday coverage to respond to student emergencies and incidents.  Assist in campus retention and student success initiatives through relationship-building, outreach, and early intervention.   Collaboration and University Engagement     Support the development and implementation of residential learning communities in collaboration with faculty and staff.  Collaborate with campus partners to enhance academic support, wellness initiatives, and identity-based programming within the residence halls.  Participate in departmental recruitment, selection, training, and evaluation processes for student and professional staff.  Collect and analyze data on housing trends, student satisfaction, and program outcomes to inform strategic decisions.  Recommend policy improvements in support of student success and retention. Foster relationships with key campus partners, including Academic Advising, Student Health & Wellbeing, Student Conduct, Student Engagement, and Career Services.  Collaborate with the Office of Engagement to promote, support, and actively participate in campus events and initiatives that enhance student involvement and community building.   Contribute to housing and residence life-wide initiatives, planning, assessment, and special projects.  Serve as a member of university-wide committees and emergency response teams. Perform other duties as assigned by the Assistant Dean of Residence Life & Student Development. Duties and Responsibilities: This is a full-time, 12-month, live-in position.  Evening and weekend On-Call Duty Rotation Responsibilities Required.  Benefits include meal plan, health insurance, and professional development support. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Knowledge of The Housing Director (THD) through StarRez software, Adobe Suite, and proficient in using Microsoft Office suite.  Excellent organizational and time-management skills.  Strong analytical and problem-solving skills.  Must prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment, develop communication strategies and work with internal and external stakeholders.  Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills.  Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with high accuracy.  Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information.  Ability to adapt to a flexible schedule that includes evening and weekend activities. Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Minimum one year experience in University Residence Life and Housing or Student Activities is required.  Experience in student accountability and an awareness of emerging issues in higher education, residence life and housing, community building, and student learning. Proven competence in leadership and supervision of students and staff.   Preferred Qualifications: Master’s preferred in student personnel, student development, or related field) Physical Demands: Light sedentary office work.  Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer.  Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201854144156_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "HR Administrative Support Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: The Human Resources (HR) Administrative Support Specialist provides exceptional customer service to faculty, staff, and applicants while supporting the daily operations of the Human Resources department. This position coordinates and administers a wide range of HR functions, including recruitment, onboarding, employee records management, benefits administration, and HR communications. The specialist ensures compliance with organizational policies and employment regulations, maintains accurate and confidential personnel records, and serves as a primary point of contact for HR-related inquiries. Success in this role requires strong organizational skills, attention to detail, professionalism, and the ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment while fostering a positive and service-oriented experience for employees. Essential Job Functions, Duties & Responsibilities: Provides support to a variety of HR functions, which may include talent acquisition, people engagement and learning and development.  Coordinate meetings and events, communicating with customers, administrative support, utilizing applicable technology platforms and providing regular reporting.   Provides customer service to both internal and external customers by answering phones, responding to inquiries and questions.    Completes projects, which may require researching information and presenting findings.  Provides back up and support to other functions.   Assist with tracking and implementation of HR projects.  Supports the hiring process for staff and faculty vacancies. Partners with the Talent Acquisition Manager to assist in job postings, background checks and fulfilling hiring needs as needed.    Participates in the new hire process, reviewing and processing required forms, responding to inquiries, providing new employee orientation information as requested, and ensuring consistent onboarding experience for staff and faculty.  Responsible for human resource reporting including HR metrics.    Maintains highly confidential records, including vendor contracts and agreements.  Process, scan, file and store HR information and documents.  Assists in delivering HR sponsored recognition events, service awards, and other ceremonies.  Performs other duties as assigned.  Knowledge, Skills & Abilities: Proficient in the use of Microsoft Office suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint) with an intermediate level in excel. • Possesses service-oriented mindset.    Experience with using and reporting from HRIS or learning management platforms.   Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with a high level of accuracy.  High degree of organization, ability to engage in a variety of tasks simultaneously, and consistently meet deadlines.  Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information. Positive attitude.   Minimum Qualifications: High School Diploma or equivalent and at least one year of related experience.   Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's Degree from an accredited college or university in a related field is preferred.    Physical Demands: Nature of work requires an ability to operate standard business office equipment.   Requires ability to communicate and exchange information, collects, compiles, compares, and prepares work documents, set-up and maintain work files.  Must be able to lift up to 25 lbs.  Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201854139203_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Music & Assistant Band Director",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: The Assistant Professor of Music/Assistant Band Director serves as a faculty member and administrative leader within the Division of Student Affairs and Division of Academic Affairs, supporting the artistic, educational, and operational excellence of Lincoln University's band program.  Reporting to the Director of Bands, this position provides leadership in the instruction, administration, recruitment, performance, and continued development of the University's band ensembles while contributing to the University's mission of student success, leadership development, and community engagement.  The Assistant Professor/Assistant Band Director assists with all aspects of the Marching Musical Storm, concert bands, pep bands, jazz ensembles, athletic bands, and other musical groups as assigned.  The position collaborates with students, faculty, staff, alumni, community partners, and prospective students to cultivate an environment rooted in excellence, accountability, discipline, and pride.  As a faculty member, the Assistant Professor teaches undergraduate music courses consistent with departmental needs while advising students, supporting assessment efforts, participating in institutional service, and contributing to curriculum development. Within Student Affairs, the position plays an important role in fostering student engagement, belonging, retention, leadership development, and school spirit through musical performance and campus traditions.  Through collaborative leadership, innovative recruitment strategies, and student-centered mentorship, the Assistant Professor/Assistant Band Director advances the University's commitment to developing musicians, scholars, and leaders who positively represent Lincoln University throughout Missouri and beyond.  Essential Job Functions: Assist the Director of Bands with the artistic, administrative, and operational leadership of all university band ensembles.    Conduct rehearsals and performances for the Marching Musical Storm and other instrumental ensembles as assigned.    Serve as primary conductor for designated ensembles in the absence of the Director of Bands.    Teach undergraduate music courses consistent with academic qualifications and departmental needs.   Assist with planning, coordinating, and executing all marching band performances, athletic events, concerts, ceremonies, community performances, and university special events.    Coordinate music selection, drill design implementation, rehearsal planning, and performance preparation.   Recruit prospective students through high school visits, honor bands, recruitment events, campus visits, and community outreach initiatives.    Mentor students academically, musically, and professionally while promoting student success, persistence, and graduation.    Assist with band camp planning, orientation, leadership development, and student training programs.   Support student leadership development within the band program by advising drum majors, section leaders, and student officers.  Coordinate equipment inventory, instrument maintenance, uniforms, facilities, and transportation logistics. Assist with purchasing, budgeting, vendor coordination, and fiscal management in accordance with university policies.   Maintain accurate records regarding student participation, scholarship eligibility, inventories, travel documentation, and program assessment.   Collaborate with Athletics, Student Engagement, Admissions, Alumni Affairs, Advancement, University Marketing, and Academic Affairs to support institutional initiatives.   Represent the University and Division of Student Affairs at recruitment events, alumni functions, conferences, and community engagements.    Support assessment efforts by collecting and analyzing data related to recruitment, retention, student learning outcomes, and program effectiveness.    Maintain compliance with University, NCAA, conference, state, and federal policies affecting student organizations and performances.   Participate in evening, weekend, holiday, and travel responsibilities associated with performances, competitions, recruitment, and special events.   Perform additional duties as assigned in support of the Division of Student Affairs.  Knowledge, Skills, & Abilities: Demonstrated expertise in instrumental music instruction and ensemble conducting.   Knowledge of HBCU band traditions and collegiate marching band operations.   Strong leadership, organizational, and project management skills.    Excellent verbal, written, and interpersonal communication abilities.  Demonstrated commitment to student development and holistic education.    Ability to build collaborative relationships across academic and student affairs divisions.    Strong recruitment, mentoring, and advising skills.   Ability to organize multiple priorities while meeting deadlines.    Knowledge of music technology, notation software, Microsoft Office Suite, and student information systems.    Ability to manage budgets, inventories, purchasing processes, and university resources responsibly.   Commitment to professionalism, ethical conduct, accountability, and continuous improvement.    Ability to foster an inclusive and student-centered learning environment. Thorough proficiency with Adobe Suite, Microsoft Office suite, and the ability to learn and integrate additional platforms or technologies as needed.  Knowledge of federal and state regulations impacting student mental health and higher education, including HIPAA, Title IX, Title VII, the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), the Clery Act, the Drug-Free Schools and Communities Act (DFSCA), and Office for Civil Rights (OCR) guidance and compliance requirements. Ability to adapt to a flexible schedule, including participation in evening and weekend activities, as required to meet student needs and ensure continuity of services.  Qualifications: Master's degree in music, Music Education, Instrumental Conducting, or closely related discipline from an accredited institution. Demonstrated experience as an Assistant Band Director, Band Director, Graduate Assistant, or equivalent collegiate or secondary-level leadership experience. Demonstrated experience conducting concert and/or marching ensembles. Ability to teach undergraduate music courses. Valid driver's license with ability to travel. Ability to work evenings, weekends, and extended hours during band seasons. Physical Demands: Ability to stand for extended periods during rehearsals and performances.   Ability to conduct ensembles and demonstrate instrumental techniques.    Ability to lift and transport musical instruments and equipment weighing up to 50 pounds.   Ability to travel for performances, recruitment activities, and conferences.  Ability to work outdoors during marching rehearsals and performances in varying weather conditions. Terms of Employment  Full-time, 12-month administrative faculty appointment.    Faculty rank of Assistant Professor of Music, consistent with university qualifications and promotion guidelines.   Benefits include professional development support, conference travel, and opportunities for scholarly and creative activity. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
