    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738423811_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of AI Technology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nPurpose: The Lincoln University of Missouri is seeking highly motivated, creative individuals to apply for an Assistant Professor position of Artificial Intelligence and with a focus on precision agriculture technology applications. The incumbent will conduct research on AI technology and its AG applications. This is a 12-month, non-tenure track, full-benefit eligible, 100% research faculty position at the College of Agriculture, Environmental, and Human Sciences. Essential Job Functions, Duties, and Responsibilities: Develop a high impact, nationally recognized AI research program to address the critical issues in food and agriculture. Conduct innovative field and lab research and outreach activities focusing on precision agriculture technology, crop and soil monitoring, livestock management, sensor technology, big data analytics, or related areas. Write grant proposals for extramural, competitive funding to support AI research and outreach programs. Publish scientific research findings in peer-reviewed journals and present findings at relevant professional conferences. Collaborate with faculty within the University and other research institutions to address critical issues in AI technology and applications as related to food and agriculture. Work closely with the University's research farms, extension educators, and public-private partners. May be required to teach AI-related courses, supervise undergraduate/graduate students, and provide departmental, college and university services as assigned. Other duties as assigned by the Dean or Associate Research Director. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Demonstrated ability to conduct quality research, publish research articles in peer-reviewed journals and secure extramural funding through grant proposals. Strong interpersonal skills with demonstrated verbal and written communication skills. Self-motivated with excellent organizational skills and attention to detailed. A collaborative mindset, valuing different perspectives and working effectively in a collaborative environment. Qualifications: Ph.D. in Computer Science, Data Science, or a closely related field with a focus on Artificial Intelligence and its applications in precision agriculture technology. Postdoctoral research experience in applying AI techniques to agricultural systems. Preferred Qualifications: Proficiency in AI tools and frameworks with a focus on agriculture applications. Experience with big data analytics techniques (e.g., machine learning, statistical modeling, predictive analysis, and data mining). Scientific journal publications in relevant fields. Ability to work both independently and in a team environment. Physical Demands: Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to drive vehicles and must have a valid driver's license. Ability to lift moderately heavy objects. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738422249_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Agronomist (State Extension Specialist – Row Crop Systems)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nPURPOSE: Lincoln University Cooperative Extension (LUCE), an 1890 Land-Grant Institution, seeks a highly motivated Agronomist (State Extension Specialist – Row Crop Systems) to provide statewide leadership in agronomic education and outreach. The Agronomist will design, implement, and evaluate science-based extension programs in row crop systems, including corn, soybeans, small grains, and cover crops, with an emphasis on sustainability, soil health, and profitability for Missouri producers. This position will serve as a statewide subject-matter expert, working collaboratively with extension educators and  specialists, research faculty, USDA agencies, and commodity groups to translate agronomic research into practical,  accessible recommendations that strengthen agricultural productivity, environmental stewardship, and economic  resilience, particularly among limited-resource and historically underserved farmers.  This is a 12-month Assistant  Professor, non-tenure track, full benefit-eligible position, with 100% extension responsibilities located on the main  Campus at Jefferson City, MO. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Provide statewide leadership in agronomy and row crop systems, including soil fertility, nutrient management, and sustainable cropping practices. Identify statewide needs and emerging challenges in row crop production through assessments and stakeholder engagement. Develop and deliver innovative educational programs through field demonstrations, workshops, webinars, and training events. Create and disseminate extension materials such as fact sheets, bulletins, manuals, and multimedia resources. Collaborate with the university and external partners to integrate applied agronomic research findings into educational outreach. ADDITIONAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Support adoption of soil health practices, cover cropping, conservation tillage, and integrated pest management (IPM). Mentor and support extension educators, program associates, and student interns in agronomy programming. Evaluate program outcomes and document impacts using established extension accountability systems. Seek external funding (grants, contracts, industry support) to expand agronomic extension initiatives. Represent Lincoln University in state and regional meetings, professional conferences, and stakeholder coalitions. Participate in professional development and serve as a resource to state and regional agricultural networks. Comply with all Extension federal, state, and university reporting requirements. Perform other duties as assigned by Extension Administrator(s). QUALIFICATIONS: Ph.D. in Agronomy, Crop Science, Soil Science, or a closely related field from an accredited institution.  Demonstrated experience with field crop production systems, particularly corn, soybean, and small grains. Documented success in designing and delivering educational or extension programs for agricultural audiences. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, including the ability to translate scientific information into practical recommendations. Demonstrated commitment to serving diverse and historically underserved producers consistent with the 1890 Land-Grant mission. Valid driver's license and willingness to travel extensively within Missouri. Preferred Qualifications: Experience leading statewide or multi-county extension programs in agronomy or crop systems. Familiarity with Missouri and Midwestern cropping environments, soils, and production challenges. Proven ability to secure and manage extramural funding. Demonstrated record of professional collaboration and teamwork across agencies and stakeholder groups. Experience working within a Land-Grant University, preferably an 1890 institution. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong technical foundation in crop and soil management. Proven leadership and organizational skills. Ability to engage with diverse audiences using culturally responsive and inclusive programming approaches. Skill in program evaluation and impact reporting. Proficiency with digital platforms and tools for outreach and communication. Working Conditions: Frequent travel across Missouri for farm visits, field days, and regional meetings. Occasional evening or weekend programming. Field work in variable outdoor conditions as needed. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: The position involves fieldwork in all weather conditions and at weekends. Lifting or carrying objects (up to 20 lbs.) Farm equipment and crop restraining and handling. Possible exposure to pesticides, allergens, and animal waste. REFERENCES: Contact information for three professional references. This job description is not intended to be a complete list of all responsibilities, duties or skills required for the job and is subject to review and change at any time, with or without notice, in accordance with the needs of Lincoln University. Since no job description can detail all the duties and responsibilities that may be required from time to time in the performance of a job, duties and responsibilities that may be inherent in a job, reasonably required for its performance, or required due to the changing nature of the job shall also be considered part of the jobholder's responsibility.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738418566_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Environmental Services Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY: The Environmental Services Supervisor is responsible for overseeing the daily custodial operations of assigned campus facilities and supervising environmental services staff. This position ensures buildings are clean, safe, and properly maintained while supporting service quality, staff productivity, and customer satisfaction. The supervisor serves as a working leader who coordinates schedules, inspections, training, and day-to-day operational needs. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Operations Oversight Supervise daily custodial operations in assigned buildings and areas. Assign work tasks and monitor completion of scheduled duties. Conduct routine inspections to ensure cleanliness and service standards are met. Coordinate event setups, special cleaning projects, and emergency response needs. Report maintenance issues, safety concerns, and facility deficiencies. Staff Supervision Lead, train, and support environmental services staff. Monitor attendance, productivity, and performance expectations. Assist with onboarding and ongoing staff training. Provide coaching, corrective action documentation, and performance feedback. Ensure proper shift coverage and communicate staffing needs to management. Supplies & Equipment Monitor inventory of cleaning supplies and restock as needed. Ensure custodial equipment is used properly and maintained in good condition. Report repair or replacement needs for tools and equipment. Safety & Compliance Enforce safe work practices and proper chemical handling procedures. Ensure compliance with institutional policies and applicable safety standards. Maintain clean and organized storage and janitorial closets. Customer Service Respond to service requests and resolve issues in a timely manner. Maintain positive working relationships with campus departments, students, and staff. Communicate operational updates and concerns to the Manager of Environmental Services. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES: Leadership and team development. Operational efficiency and process improvement. Problem-solving and decision-making. Attention to detail and quality assurance. Time management and prioritization. Strong communication, leadership, and organizational skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities and staff assignments. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: High school diploma or equivalent. 3+ years of custodial, housekeeping, or environmental services experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: 1+ year of supervisory or lead experience preferred. Experience in higher education, healthcare, or large-scale facilities. Knowledge of custodial equipment and modern cleaning technologies. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Prolonged standing and walking across campus facilities for inspections and operational support. Ability to lift, carry, push, or pull up to 50 lbs., including equipment and supplies. Frequent bending, stooping, kneeling, and reaching (overhead and below waist). Operation of custodial equipment and carts, including pushing/pulling loaded equipment. Exposure to cleaning chemicals, dust, and varying indoor/outdoor environmental conditions. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738418337_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Environmental Services Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY: The Environmental Services Technician is responsible for performing routine custodial and sanitation services to maintain campus facilities in a clean, safe, and welcoming condition. This position supports the daily cleaning needs of academic, residential, administrative, and public spaces while providing quality customer service to students, faculty, staff, and visitors. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Cleaning & Sanitation Clean and sanitize restrooms, classrooms, offices, hallways, common areas, and other assigned spaces. Sweep, mop, vacuum, dust, and remove trash/recycling from facilities. Refill restroom and cleaning supplies as needed. Clean glass, doors, furniture, and high-touch surfaces. Perform floor care tasks such as buffing, scrubbing, and spot cleaning. Operations Support Set up and break down rooms for meetings, events, and campus activities. Report maintenance issues, damages, or safety concerns to supervisors. Respond to service requests in a timely manner. Support seasonal or special cleaning projects as assigned. Safety & Compliance Follow proper chemical handling and cleaning procedures. Use custodial equipment safely and effectively. Maintain clean and organized janitorial closets and storage areas. Follow all institutional safety policies and procedures. Customer Service Interact professionally with students, faculty, staff, and visitors. Represent the department in a courteous and helpful manner. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES: Problem-solving and decision-making. Attention to detail and quality assurance. Time management and prioritization. QUALIFICATIONS: High school diploma or equivalent preferred. Ability to follow directions and work independently or as part of a team. Reliable attendance and strong work ethic. Basic communication and time management skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Prior custodial, housekeeping, or environmental services experience. Experience operating custodial equipment and performing floor care. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Prolonged standing and walking across campus facilities for inspections and operational support. Ability to lift, carry, push, or pull up to 50 lbs., including equipment and supplies. Frequent bending, stooping, kneeling, and reaching (overhead and below waist). Operation of custodial equipment and carts, including pushing/pulling loaded equipment. Exposure to cleaning chemicals, dust, and varying indoor/outdoor environmental conditions. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738422742_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Project Specialist & CARE Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary:  The Project Specialist and CARE  Coordinator for Student Affairs is responsible for performing various administrative functions, including the  performance of special assignments for the Chief Student Affairs Officer and supporting the CARE Team with  case management and student support initiatives.  Coordinate Student Affairs activities delegated, including following up on information requests, compiling data and other materials, and ensuring compliance with internal and external deadlines. Prepare PCRs/PTRs, travel requests, and travel reimbursement forms. Make air transportation arrangements for Student Affairs staff. Monitor divisional expenditures throughout the fiscal year and recommend budget adjustments as appropriate. Maintenance of Records Management for the Office of the Chief Student Affairs Officer, assuring all information is current and disposed of properly. Composes correspondence, memorandum, reports, and other office documents from rough drafts and oral instructions for the signatures of the Chief Student Affairs Officer. This includes preparing the Student Affairs BOC and Annual Reports and other documents in compliance with agreed-upon timelines. Assigned work is often confidential. Maintains Chief Student Affairs Officer calendar of appointments and coordinates arrangements for university meetings and conferences, plans meetings and conferences with outside agencies, room reservations, and room setups, and menu preparation for breakfast and luncheon meetings. Informs participants of agenda items, provides background information as needed, sends reminders and follow-up information to participants, and provides general clerical support for these functions. Acts as office manager for the Office of the Chief Student Affairs Officer. Receives incoming mail, phone calls, and visitors for the Chief Student Affairs Officer screens and forwards those items, not requiring the Chief Student Affairs Officer attention. Prepare operational forms as necessary to ensure the effective functioning of the office. Act as the primary point of contact for students referred to the CARE Team, providing case management services and assessing students' needs. Facilitate connections between students and essential on-campus and off-campus resources, such as mental health services, financial aid, and housing assistance. Monitor student progress and follow up on referrals to ensure timely interventions and support.  Collaborate with Residential Life and Housing departments to address housing insecurity, providing interventions to maintain stability for at-risk students. Advocate for students' needs within the university, ensuring their concerns are understood by faculty, staff, and stakeholders. Participate in weekly CARE Team meetings to discuss student well-being and safety concerns. Document all case interactions in compliance with institutional guidelines and FERPA regulations, contributing to the ongoing improvement of student care practices. Maintain regular communication with Residential Life staff and Student Health & Well-being staff regarding student housing concerns and support the transition of students returning from medical withdrawals or crises. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in social work, Psychology, Sociology, or related field from an accredited institution required. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Knowledge and experience in Microsoft Office programs. Experience as an administrative assistant, case management, or in a clerical support position demonstrating  confidentiality, organization skills and management of multiple priorities. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities and Personal Characteristics: Thorough knowledge of Adobe Suite and Proficient in using Microsoft Office suite (Word, Excel, PowerPoint). Must prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment, develop communication strategies and work with internal and external stakeholders. Must understand the many facets of marketing at a dynamic university. Excellent interpersonal and verbal communication skills. Excellent organizational and time-management skills. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Ability to work within a culturally diverse environment. Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with high accuracy. The high degree of organization, ability to engage in various tasks simultaneously, and consistently meet deadlines. Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information. Be team oriented. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: The nature of work requires an ability to operate standard business office equipment. Light sedentary office work. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Must be able to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738425717_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Senior Director of Student Health",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY: The Senior Director of Student Health and Well-being is tasked with providing strategic leadership and direction for comprehensive health and wellness services for students. This includes overseeing medical services, mental health counseling, health education, and wellness programs. The Senior Director will ensure that these services are culturally relevant and supportive of the unique needs of students. This role is critical in fostering a healthy campus environment that promotes the physical, mental, and emotional well-being of students. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS: Hire, onboard, train, evaluate, and supervise departmental staff, including support for student employees. Provide visionary leadership and strategic direction for the Office of Student Health and Well-being. Supervise and evaluate health and counseling staff, including hiring, training, and professional development. Develop and manage departmental budgets, ensuring efficient use of resources. Oversee the delivery of high-quality medical services, mental health counseling, and health education programs. Ensure compliance with state, federal regulations, and accreditation standards for health services by implementing policies and procedures that align with the University's mission, vision, and values. Develop and implement health and wellness programs that address the specific needs of the student population. Promote a holistic approach to student health and well-being, integrating physical, mental, and emotional health services. Create initiatives that enhance students' health literacy and empower them to make informed health decisions. Provide crisis intervention and support for students experiencing health or wellness-related emergencies. Establish and maintain collaborative relationships with campus partners, including academic departments, student organizations, and community health resources. Coordinate outreach efforts to engage students in health and wellness programs. Serve as a resource for faculty and staff on student health and well-being issues. Develop and implement assessment tools to measure the effectiveness of health and wellness programs, utilizing data to inform decision-making and enhance services, while preparing regular reports on program outcomes and student health trends. Design and deliver educational workshops, seminars, and campaigns on health and well-being topics, fostering a campus culture that values and supports wellness, and encouraging student involvement in health promotion and peer education programs. QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree in public health, Health Administration, Psychology, State licensure as a Nurse Practitioner/ Physician Assistant, Student Personnel, or related field. Experience and ability to manage budgets. 3 - 5 years' experience in a college/university setting related to student health and well-being. Commitment to promoting equity, diversity, and inclusion in health and wellness programs. Knowledge of relevant state and federal health regulations and accreditation standards.  Knowledge, Skills, Abilities and Personal Characteristics: Thorough knowledge of student health software, Adobe Suite and proficient in using Microsoft Office suite. Excellent organizational and time-management skills.  Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. Must prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment, develop communication strategies and work with internal and external stakeholders. Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills. Ability to work within a culturally diverse environment. Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with high accuracy. A high degree of organization, ability to engage in various tasks simultaneously, and consistently meet deadlines. Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information. Ability to adapt to a flexible schedule that includes evening and weekend activities. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Light sedentary office work. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201632482657_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Title III Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY: The Title III Coordinator provides administrative and programmatic support to the Title III office, including coordinating and administering all Title II grant-funded activities, monitoring project activities and compliance tracking. Other responsibilities include training, process improvement, assisting with preparing Annual Performance Reports and grant applications, and ensuring compliance with federal regulations. This position provides direct support to the Senior Director of Sponsored Programs, Title III, and Project Directors and serves as a liaison between internal stakeholders and grant activity leaders to ensure efficient and effective implementation of Title II program activities. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS, DUTIES & RESPONSIBILITIES: Coordinate and monitor all Title III grant-funded initiatives to ensure compliance with federal regulations and institutional guidance. Coordinate scheduling and travel arrangements for Title III Professional Development and track expenditures. Maintain organized travel and financial records to support accurate reporting. Draft routine correspondence, edit and format official communications, reports, and presentations. Provide administrative and procedural support to Title III Project Directors to promote compliance with federal regulations and institutional policies. Review project documentation and progress reports to monitor performance and assist in meeting program goals. Work with Project Directors to develop measurable objectives and performance indicators that align with federal guidelines. Design and deliver training and resources to strengthen understanding of Title III processes and compliance requirements. Collect, verify, and compile quarterly reports and update objectives and performance measures from Project Directors for institutional and federal reporting and assist with annual performance reports. Prepare reports and support materials for the Senior Director of Title III during audits and grant compliance review. Draft and compile materials for federal submissions, including Annual Performance Reports and Title III grant applications. Track project activities and outcomes to support accurate reporting and performance evaluations. Monitor and stays informed of changes in federal grant policies and requirements affecting Title III operations. Build and maintain positive working relationships with Title III staff, faculty, and administrators to support collaboration and program success. Performs other duties as assigned. Assumes additional responsibilities and performs special projects as needed or requested. Exhibits student-centeredness in the performance of all job duties. Treats all others with respect, understands the impact of culture/background on the behavior of others, respects differences among the Lincoln community and demonstrates inclusive behavior. Anticipates, listens to, understands, and responds to customer's needs. Delivers work products and services to customers in a way that reflects positively upon the department and the University. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES: Potential to work effectively with the culture and values of Lincoln University and able to work comfortably within a diverse and shared services organizational model. Extensive broad-based knowledge of principles, practices, techniques of oral and written communications and interpersonal skills to determine the most effective method to present persuasive proposals to potential funding sources. Knowledge of federal research funding agencies and their associated guidelines. Strong writing, planning, organizational, and management skills. Strong communication skills include the ability to write and speak persuasively about the university. Strong ability to prioritize multiple projects, meeting strict deadlines, while maintaining strong attention to detail. Excellent knowledge and experience of computer applications such as word processing, excel, electronic data tracking, internet, websites, Facebook, etc. Flexibility and initiative with the ability to work independently as well as in a team environment. Ability to understand a variety of research disciplines, gained through experience and/or training or an openness to learning new disciplines to best assist faculty and staff. Knowledge of budgets and financial management for grants. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree, three or more years of experience in project management, program oversight, grant administration, or directly related/similar experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: 5 years of experience in grants, sponsored programs, or Title III. Professional experience in an environment, such as a university or non-profit agency. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Light sedentary office work. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to lift 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738416299_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Senior Payroll Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-08\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY: The Senior Payroll Specialist is responsible for the accurate production and timely delivery of two monthly payrolls (exempt & non-exempt salaried, nonexempt hourly (including students). Understands the ADP payroll system to accurately pay employees in compliance with federal and state laws and regulations, such as the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) rules and regulations and the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA). Assures proper tax treatment, accounting, and disposition of withholdings, such as taxes, deductions for benefits, charitable contributions, retirement contributions, voluntary deductions, etc.  Works closely with the Human Resource Services, Accounting/Finance Department, IT Department, and other University departments as well as the state retirement offices, Internal Revenue Service, Social Security Administration, and others receiving the proceeds from payroll deductions.  Will also be responsible for data entry of payroll records for all full-time, part-time and student employees and maintaining employee files related to payroll as well as presenting new hire orientation information pertaining to payroll related material. May make electronic payments and upload electronic files to various vendors. Will be cross trained as primary backup to  Payroll Supervisor. ESSENTIAL JOB FUNCTIONS, DUTIES, & RESPONSIBILITIES: Ensures proper payment of wages and salaries by maintaining and/or supervising the data entry and preparation of two monthly payrolls, assuring that timecards are correctly processing salaries and reported hours worked, correctly utilizing paid leave and holiday pay, and correctly computing overtime. Reviews wages computed and corrected errors to ensure accuracy of payroll. Records changes affecting net wages such as exemptions, insurance coverage, and other deductions for each employee to update master payroll records.  Adjusts payroll and other benefits or deductions as needed. Reconciles and processes monthly payments for insurance, retirement, garnishments, etc. Balances accounts and communicates with outside vendors for accuracy of billing and payments.  Monitors control procedures related to the preparation and disbursement of direct deposits and of payroll checks. Prepares and ensures accurate and timely completion of all payroll tax reporting requirements to federal, state and local governments, including quarterly reporting and issuances of annual Form W-2s. Ensures that the ADP payroll system is working as expected with regular delivered software updates/maintenance and is in compliance with federal and state laws, rules, and regulations by establishing controls, monitoring results, and collaborating with Human Resource Services and IT Services to modify, update and adapt programming, processing methods, and reporting in response to changes in regulations, withholding rates, reporting needs, etc. Generates records of each payroll showing each payee, hours worked for nonexempt employees, wages paid, associated withholdings for retirement, benefits, and other contributions, and taxes withheld, supervises the maintenance of payroll records and accounts. Review data entry and maintain employee information from PTR's (Personnel Transaction Records), SER's (Student Employment Records) and CWS (College Work-Study) student forms. Maintain and present payroll related material for new hire orientations. Answers questions from employees concerning payroll questions either via telephone or in person. Works with Accounting/Finance Office personnel to coordinate required accounting processes for monthly, quarterly and yearly reporting.  Performs various journal entries, and account reconciliations related to payroll. As a part of Human Resources department, also maintaining knowledge and a general understanding of HR processes and procedures, providing coverage, answering routine questions as necessary. Responds to payroll audit requests. Other duties as assigned. KNOWLEDGE, SKILLS, & ABILITIES: Ability to write reports, business correspondence, and procedure manuals as well as effectively communicate orally (in person and by telephone) and in writing. Ability to solve practical problems and deal with a variety of variables in situations where only limited standardization exists. Ability to multi-task yet maintain close attention to detail and timeliness of work production. Ability to use discretion and judgment and work independently while maintaining highest level of confidentiality. Ability to work in a positive team-oriented manner with a variety of people (students, faculty, staff, administrators, vendors and members of the public), including disgruntled individuals. Requires the ability to use small office equipment, including desktop calculator, copy machines or multi-line telephone systems as well as the ability to use computers for data entry, word processing and/or accounting purposes. A record of inclusive conduct and evidence of multicultural skills in the workplace is preferred. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: High School diploma or GED required. Three years of progressively responsible in-house payroll accounting management experience is required including demonstrated knowledge of accounting principles, payroll methods, Department of Labor regulations, federal and state tax regulations, and local and international tax requirements. Proficiency with Excel and/or other spreadsheet and database applications. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Bachelor's degree in a business-related field and/or twenty hours of coursework in accounting. Supervisory experience. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Light sedentary office work. Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to lift and/or move up to 25 lbs. Specific vision abilities required by this job include close perception and distance vision as related to multiple desktop computer screens, etc. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201738430198_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Animal Science/Nutritionist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-01\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJOB SUMMARY:  Lincoln University is seeking highly motivated, creative individuals to apply for the Assistant Professor position in Animal Science with the focus on small ruminant nutrition. This is a full-time, non-tenure track, full benefit-eligible faculty position with 75% research and 25% extension/outreach appointments. Essential Job Functions: Develop a high-impact, nationally recognized, animal nutrition-focused research program to address critical or emergent issues relevant to the small ruminant industry throughout Missouri and beyond. Conduct innovative laboratory and field research as well as science-backed educational outreach that aligns with producers' needs. Write grant proposals for extramural, competitive funding to support small ruminant nutrition research program. Publish research findings in peer-reviewed journals and contribute to relevant professional societies. Collaborate with faculty within the University and other institutions. Work closely with University's research farms, extension educators, small ruminant producers, and industry partners. May be required to teach courses relevant to their expertise, supervise undergraduate and graduate students, and provide departments, college, and university services as assigned. Other duties as assigned by the Dean or Associate Research Director. Knowledge, Skills, & Abilities: Demonstrated ability to conduct quality research, publish research articles in peer-reviewed journals, and secure extramural funding through grant proposals. Strong interpersonal skills with demonstrated verbal and written communication skills. Self-motivated with excellent organizational skills and attention to detail. A collaborative mindset, valuing different perspectives and working effectively in a collaborative environment. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Animal Science with a focus on Animal Nutrition, Animal Physiology, or a closely related field. Background in experimental design, data management, and statistical analysis is required. Demonstrated expertise and experience in animal science (animal nutrition) is required. Preferred Qualifications: Experience of postdoctoral research in animal nutrition/physiology. Publication record in peer-reviewed journals. Familiarity with critical and emergent issues facing small ruminant industry. Physical Demands: Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to drive vehicles and must have a valid driver's license. Ability to lift up to 25 lbs. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9202112587810_3&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "HR Generalist – Employee Relations and Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nPosition: HR Generalist – Employee Relations and Engagement Job Summary: The HR Generalist - Employee Relations and Engagement is a cross-functional Human Resources professional with primary responsibility for supporting and coordinating employee relations and employee engagement efforts across the University. Serving as a lead resource for day-to-day employee relations matters, the position provides guidance to employees and supervisors, supports workplace issue resolution, conducts or assists with fact-finding and investigations, promotes consistent application of University policies and practices, and helps identify opportunities to strengthen the employee experience and workplace culture.  The position also has a significant role in facilitating HR-related learning, orientation, compliance education, supervisor development, and employee engagement programming in coordination with HR leadership and appropriate University partners. As a fully cross-trained HR Generalist, the incumbent is expected to maintain working knowledge across Human Resources functions and provide hands-on assistance and backup support in recruitment, onboarding, benefits and leave administration, HRIS transactions, records, compliance, employee lifecycle processes, and other HR operations as departmental needs require.  Essential Job Functions, Duties, & Responsibilities: Serves as a lead day-to-day HR resource for employee relations matters, working under the direction of the Executive Director & Chief Human Resources Officer and escalating high-risk, complex, or legally sensitive matters as appropriate.  Provides guidance to supervisors and employees regarding workplace concerns, performance matters, attendance, conduct, conflict resolution, corrective action processes, and interpretation of university policies and HR practices.  Conducts or assists with prompt, objective, and well-documented fact-finding and workplace investigations; prepares interview notes, case documentation, findings summaries, and related records in accordance with established HR processes.  Supports consistent and equitable administration of employee relations practices by reviewing documentation, identifying policy or process considerations, recommending next steps to HR leadership, and tracking matters through resolution.  Maintains confidential employee relations files, case notes, investigation materials, corrective action records, and related documentation with appropriate discretion and records management practices.  Identifies recurring employee relations themes and operational concerns and provides HR leadership with observations and recommendations for policy clarification, supervisor support, communication, or preventive education.  Coordinates employee engagement initiatives that support connection, recognition, communication, retention, and a positive workplace culture, including surveys, listening opportunities, events, programs, and follow-up activities as assigned.  Assists with gathering and reviewing employee feedback and engagement information and supports development of practical action plans in partnership with HR leadership and University stakeholders.  Facilitates HR-related training, workshops, and informational sessions for supervisors, employees, and other campus audiences on topics within assigned expertise, such as employee relations practices, performance management, HR processes, workplace expectations, policy awareness and compliance topics.  Develops and updates HR training materials, job aids, presentations, guides, and related resources in coordination with HR leadership and other University subject-matter owners to promote accurate, consistent information and avoid duplication of institutional training responsibilities.  Supports scheduling, communication, documentation, attendance tracking, and evaluation of assigned HR learning activities and collaboration with appropriate University offices on broader professional development or training initiatives.  Maintains sufficient cross-functional knowledge to perform the core work assigned to HR Generalists and provides backup or surge support across Human Resources based on service needs, staffing levels, deadlines, and departmental priorities.  Assists with recruitment and hiring activities, including requisition and posting support, applicant and hiring manager communication, screening coordination, interview processes, selection documentation, pre-employment requirements, and onboarding.  Supports employee onboarding and lifecycle transactions, including new-hire processing, employment documentation, HRIS data entry, position or personnel changes, transfers, status changes, separations, and offboarding activities within assigned authority.  Assists with benefits, leave, and accommodation administration by providing routine information, collecting and routing documentation, tracking deadlines, maintaining records, and referring complex matters to the responsible HR lead or external administrator as applicable.  Supports HR records administration, audits, reporting, data quality, compliance reviews, and preparation or maintenance of HR documents and forms.  Responds to employee and supervisor questions, provides high-quality service, and routes matters requiring specialized expertise or approval to the appropriate HR team member or University office.  Maintains current working knowledge of applicable employment laws, regulations, University policies, and HR practices relevant to assigned responsibilities, and participates in professional development as directed.  Partners across the HR team to document procedures, cross-train on essential processes, improve workflows, and support continuity of HR services during vacancies, peak workload periods, or absences.  Maintains strict confidentiality and exercises sound judgment in handling employee information, investigations, medical or leave documentation, compensation information, and other sensitive records.  Performs other duties and special projects as assigned that are reasonably related to the position and the operational needs of Human Resources. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong working knowledge of employee relations principles, workplace investigations, performance management, conflict resolution, and consistent policy administration.  Working knowledge of core Human Resources functions and the ability and willingness to cross-train and provide support outside the position's primary employee relations and engagement focus.  Knowledge of employment laws and compliance requirements relevant to Human Resources, including principles associated with EEO, Title VII, ADA, ADEA, FMLA, FLSA, and other applicable federal and state requirements.  Ability to assess sensitive workplace issues objectively, gather relevant information, document facts, identify risk, and appropriately escalate matters requiring executive, legal, civil rights, or specialized review.  Ability to facilitate professional HR learning sessions, orientations, workshops, and presentations for groups with varying levels of knowledge and responsibility.  Ability to develop clear training materials, job aids, communications, and practical HR resources in consultation with subject-matter owners.  Strong interpersonal, listening, facilitation, written communication, and conflict-management skills with the ability to establish credibility across all levels of the University.  Ability to maintain confidentiality, exercise discretion, and manage emotionally sensitive or high conflict matters with professionalism and neutrality.  Strong organizational, case-management, documentation, analytical, problem-solving, and follow-through skills.  Ability to manage multiple priorities, deadlines, and employee matters while maintaining responsive service and accurate records.  Proficiency with Microsoft Office 365 and the ability to learn and effectively use HRIS, applicant tracking, case management, electronic workflow, survey, learning, and other enterprise systems used by the University.  Ability to work independently while functioning as a collaborative, cross-trained member of the Human Resources team.  Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in human resources, Business Administration, Public Administration, Organizational Development, or a related field required.  At least two to three years of progressively responsible professional Human Resources experience, including substantive experience supporting employee relations matters.  Demonstrated experience advising employees or supervisors, addressing workplace concerns, documenting employee relations matters, or supporting workplace investigations.  Demonstrated experience delivering presentations, facilitating training, orientation, workshops, or other employee learning activities. Preferred Qualifications: Professional Human Resources experience in higher education, public-sector employment, or another complex or regulated environment.  Experience independently conducting or leading workplace investigations and preparing formal investigation or employee relations documentation.  Experience designing or coordinating employee engagement, recognition, retention, culture, or employee listening initiatives.  Experience supporting multiple HR functional areas such as recruitment, onboarding, benefits, leave, HRIS/data operations, compensation, records, or compliance.  Experience developing and facilitating supervisor or employee training programs and evaluating participation or program effectiveness.  Physical Demands: Work is primarily performed in a professional office environment.  The position requires regular use of a computer, keyboard, telephone, and standard office equipment and may involve prolonged periods of sitting and repetitive keyboarding.  The role also requires the ability to communicate and facilitate presentations or training sessions for groups in person or through virtual platforms.  The employee may occasionally be required to lift or move materials weighing up to 25 pounds.  Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable qualified individuals to perform the essential functions of the position.  Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9202092625399_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Student Engagement Prog Coord & Scruggs University Center",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nStudent Engagement Program Coordinator & Scruggs University Center Manager  Job Summary: The Student Engagement Program Coordinator & Scruggs University Center Manager provides operational leadership for the Scruggs University Center and supports student organization development at Lincoln University of Missouri. Reporting to the Assistant Dean of Student Engagement, the Manager oversees facility operations, reservations, event logistics, recreational services, the campus bowling center, the Esports Lounge, and student staff. The position also helps student organizations operate effectively and creates opportunities for student involvement, leadership, and belonging.  Essential Job Functions, Duties, and Responsibilities: University Center Operations and Event Services: Oversee daily operations of the Scruggs University Center, including lounges, meeting rooms, recreational areas, and the campus bowling center.  Manage event setup and breakdown, room arrangements, equipment needs, and onsite support in accordance with event specifications and University standards to support effective use by students, faculty, staff, and community partners.  Maintain standards for safety, cleanliness, accessibility, and customer service; coordinate with Facilities and Maintenance and other campus partners to address concerns.  Monitor routine and preventive maintenance needs, submit and track service requests, and schedule technical support.  Respond promptly and professionally to student and customer questions, suggestions, concerns, and complaints.  Student Organization Support and Leadership Development: Coordinate organization registration and annual renewal; maintain current officer lists, governing documents, and required records in the designated engagement system.  Guide student leaders on group administration, campus policies, resources, and leadership responsibilities; provide temporary advising for groups awaiting an assigned advisor.  Coach organizations through event proposals, logistics, risk review, and required approvals in collaboration with campus partners.  Organize involvement fairs and leadership learning opportunities that help students discover organizations and strengthen group effectiveness.  Assess organization participation and leadership programs, recommending improvements that respond to student feedback and documented needs.  Bowling Center and Recreational Services: Oversee daily bowling center operations and coordinate maintenance of lane setter machines, scoring systems, ball returns, and related equipment with qualified service personnel. Apply United States Bowling Congress rules and other governing requirements when applicable to leagues, competitions, or sanctioned activities.  Analyze facility usage and collaborate with the Assistant Dean of Student Engagement on marketing and programming to increase participation and revenue through leagues, open play, and special events.  Promote safe equipment use and coordinate timely response to equipment malfunctions and operational concerns. Esports Lounge Operations and Programming: Oversee daily Esports Lounge operations, including hours, reservations, participant check-in, gaming stations, equipment inventory, and safe use of the space.  Coordinate equipment maintenance, software updates, network support, and troubleshooting with Information Technology and authorized vendors; maintain appropriate access and equipment security.  Develop marketing and advertising plans with Student Engagement and University Communications to promote lounge use, team recruitment, open play, and tournaments through approved campus channels.  Coordinate student esports team recruitment, rosters, practice schedules, and competition participation; support team leadership, sportsmanship, and adherence to university and competition requirements.  Plan and manage recreational and competitive tournaments, including registration, rules, brackets, scheduling, staffing, equipment, promotion, and event logistics; coordinate livestreaming when appropriate.  Maintain a welcoming gaming environment, communicate participation expectations, and address concerns about conduct or equipment use through established University procedures.  Track lounge attendance, equipment needs, team participation, tournament results, and promotional reach; recommend improvements to programming and resource use. Student Staff Supervision. Supervise assigned student workers; develop work schedules, assign responsibilities, and monitor completion of tasks.  Train student staff in customer service, facility procedures, event support, safe equipment use, and emergency protocols.  Provide regular feedback and support student employees in developing professional skills and accountability.  Collaboration and Administrative Responsibilities: Support coordinated campus programming, including orientation, nights/weekend events, and Homecoming; work with students, employees, and vendors on event delivery.  Contribute to department planning and evaluation; promote student development and respectful service across diverse student communities.  Track facility utilization, event activity, maintenance needs, and bowling center revenue; provide operational reports and recommendations to the Assistant Dean of Student Engagement.  Maintain compliance with university procedures and applicable occupational health and safety requirements; safeguard confidential information.  Serve on University committees and emergency response teams as assigned and perform other duties assigned by the Assistant Dean of Student Engagement.  Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in higher education, education, business, recreation management, or a related field; an equivalent combination of education, training, and relevant experience may be considered.   At least one year of experience in a leadership role involving student activities, programming, facility operations, or a related area.   Knowledge, Skills, Abilities and Personal Characteristics: Knowledge of Adobe Suite and proficient in using Microsoft Office suite.  Excellent organizational and time-management skills.  Strong analytical and problem-solving skills.  Must prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment, develop communication strategies and work with internal and external stakeholders.  Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills.  Strong attention to detail; ability to perform duties with high accuracy.  Ability to work with and maintain confidential University information.  Ability to adapt to a flexible schedule that includes evening and weekend activities.  Physical Demands: Light sedentary office work.  Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. Ability to lift up to 25 lbs.  Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.  Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9202112989410_8&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Campus Security",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: The Campus Security will function as a responsible team member with the Residential Life staff, Police Department, and community. Promote Safety / Security on property owned and operated by the university. Essential Job Functions, Duties, and Responsibilities: Protect the university property and staff by maintaining a safe and secure environment. Observe for signs of crime or disorder and investigate disturbances. Take accurate notes of unusual occurrences. Report in detail on any suspicious incidents. Patrol randomly or regularly the building. Monitor and control access at building entrances Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Able to pass a state background check. Excellent communication skills. Physically able to conduct foot patrols on uneven surfaces, slick or wet surfaces, stairs, rough terrain and in all weather conditions.  Perform first aid or CPR, maintain a professional, courteous, and polite demeanor, and perform any additional duties assigned by Lincoln University Police Department and Staff.  Be familiar with and carry out emergency evacuation procedures, in coordination with other Residential Life staff. Qualifications: High School Diploma / GED OR Equivalent Strong working knowledge of security operations, safety practices. Excellent surveillance and observation skills. Physical Demands: Ability to sit at a workstation for extended periods. Occasional ability to lift, carry, and put away parcels weighing up to 25 pounds. Normal physical mobility, which includes movement from place to place on the job taking distance and speed into account. Normal physical agility, which includes ability to maneuver body while in place. Normal dexterity of hands and fingers; Normal endurance. Normal coordination, including eye-hand, hand-foot. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=6105d53b-aeb8-4656-a07d-2743bd4a2423&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9202094331879_2&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Chemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\n\nLocation: Jefferson City\n\nJob Summary: Replacement faculty position in Chemistry to fill teaching needs. This is a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track position in the STM department. This position will be supporting the Master of Science in Natural Sciences and undergraduate programs including those in Chemistry, Agriculture, Nursing, Education and the various pre professional programs on campus.  Essential Job Functions, Duties, & Responsibilities: Expectations include teaching undergraduate and graduate courses in general, physical and/or organic chemistry and development of an active, externally funded research program with the ability to include undergraduate research.  Advise and mentor students and serve on departmental and institutional committees. This appointment will include an expectation of involvement in undergraduate clubs or organizations and the possibility of advising one of these organizations. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Knowledge and expertise in the assigned academic discipline or professional field. Interest in developing a research program which can incorporate undergraduate students is strongly desired.  Knowledge of effective teaching methodologies, curriculum design, assessment practices, and student learning strategies Ability to teach and support students in classroom, online, hybrid, or other instructional settings. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain accurate academic records. Ability to use instructional technology, learning management systems, and standard office software. Ability to work effectively with a diverse population of students, faculty, staff, and community stakeholders. Commitment to student learning, academic quality, professional development, and continuous improvement. Ability to maintain positive relationships within the university and the broader community. All duties pertinent to teaching, including curriculum development, mentoring, advising, participating in program assessment and review and maintaining office hours will be required.  Minimum Qualifications: Post-doctoral experience is a plus. Willingness to develop teaching, curriculum, assessment, and evaluation skills as needed. Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry, Organic Chemistry, Physical Chemistry, or a related area is required.   Preference will be given to applicants with teaching experience in a variety of chemistry areas. Previous teaching experience. Experience teaching in classroom, online, hybrid, or other instructional formats. Required academic credentials, degree, certification, license, or professional experience necessary to teach in the assigned discipline or program. Experience with curriculum development, instructional methodology, assessment, or evaluation. Physical Demands: Academic, classroom, online, office, or program-based environment. Regular interaction with students, faculty, staff, academic leadership, and university departments. May require use of online learning platforms, virtual meeting tools, instructional technology, and student information systems. May require occasional evening, weekend, or remote work depending on course schedules, program needs, and instructional format. Lincoln University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Employment decisions are based on qualifications, merit, and institutional needs. Applicants requiring reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at 573-681-5018.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
